# PART 7 - ELITE: measure QueryMesh like a research lab, then harden it like a production team
## 7.0 Who defines the top level today
What: beginners check "does it answer?". Professionals measure HOW WELL each part works, change one thing at a time, and keep the numbers. Researchers publish the methods everyone else then copies. This part shows who they are and gives you a hands-on version of their main method: an evaluation harness that scores retrieval and the whole agent pipeline.
Why: without numbers you cannot tell whether hybrid search, the grader or a new chunk size helped. With numbers you can defend every design choice in an interview ("hybrid raised hit@5 from 0.63 to 0.80 on 30 questions").
When: after Part 6A (the agents work). You do not need EKS for this part; everything runs on the Hyperstack VM.
The people, papers and organisations behind each QueryMesh idea:
§ Who § What they contributed § Where you use it in QueryMesh
§ Gordon Cormack, Charles Clarke, Stefan Buettcher (University of Waterloo), SIGIR 2009 [19] § Reciprocal Rank Fusion: merge ranked lists by adding 1/(k + rank), with k = 60 § the score-ranker-processor in search.py (rank_constant 60)
§ Akari Asai et al. (University of Washington, Allen Institute for AI), Self-RAG, 2023 [45] § the model decides when to retrieve and critiques its own output for support and usefulness § the critic agent and the writer retry loop
§ Shi-Qi Yan et al., Corrective RAG (CRAG), 2024 [45] § a light evaluator grades retrieved documents; bad results trigger query rewriting or a web search § grader -> rewrite_query -> scout loop in the supervisor
§ Shahul Es, Jithin James, Luis Espinosa-Anke, Steven Schockaert, RAGAS, 2023 [45] § reference-free RAG metrics (faithfulness, answer relevance, context relevance) and synthetic test sets § evaluate.py build (synthetic questions) and the grounded rate
§ Jon Saad-Falcon, Omar Khattab, Christopher Potts, Matei Zaharia (Stanford), ARES, 2023 [45] § trained LLM judges plus statistical confidence intervals for RAG evaluation § Section 7.4: report n and do not trust small differences
§ Omar Khattab and Matei Zaharia, ColBERT / ColBERTv2 [45] § late-interaction retrieval: one vector per token, much better ranking than one vector per chunk § the natural next step after BM25 + single-vector hybrid (Section 7.5)
§ Nelson F. Liu et al. (Stanford), Lost in the Middle, 2023 [45] § LLMs use information at the start and end of a long context best § why QueryMesh sends only the few graded chunks to the writer, not 50
§ Anthropic: Building effective agents (December 2024) and Contextual Retrieval (September 2024) [46] § start with simple, inspectable workflows (routing, evaluator-optimizer loops) before autonomous agents; prepend context to each chunk before embedding and BM25 § the deterministic supervisor policy, and the title prepended to each chunk in index_chunks
§ LangChain team (Harrison Chase and the LangGraph maintainers) [20] § LangGraph: agents as explicit state machines with checkpoints, streaming and human-in-the-loop § graph.py
§ Anthropic, Model Context Protocol (November 2024); now developed in the open with many companies (verify the current steward) [24] § a standard way for an AI application to call tools § every agent's /mcp/sse endpoint
§ Google, Agent2Agent protocol (April 2025), donated to the Linux Foundation (2025) [25] § a standard way for independent agents to discover each other (Agent Cards) and hand over tasks § the Agent Cards and the retriever -> scout delegation
§ OpenSearch Project (Linux Foundation) [14][15] § open-source hybrid search with native RRF since 2.19 § the hybrid query
§ vLLM team (UC Berkeley Sky Computing Lab and community) [21] § PagedAttention and high-throughput serving § your LLM server on Hyperstack
What the top teams do differently (and you will do now):
- They build a test set BEFORE tuning, and never tune on the final test questions.
- They score each stage separately (retrieval, grading, generation) and the whole pipeline.
- They change ONE setting per experiment and write every result in a table.
- They report the number of questions and treat small differences as noise.
- They keep the evaluation in CI, so a change that makes answers worse is caught before deploy.
## SECTION 7.1 - Create querymesh/evaluate.py
WHERE: VS Code connected to the VM, Explorer, folder ~/querymesh/querymesh
GOAL: one script with three commands: build a test set, compare search modes, measure the full agent pipeline.
Step 1. Create the file querymesh/evaluate.py with the procedure from Section 2B.3.
Step 2. Paste this content and press Ctrl+S:
@code querymesh/evaluate.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ the docstring at the top § how to run the three commands
§ `from querymesh import config, search`, `from querymesh.embeddings import embed`, `from querymesh.graph import ask`, `from querymesh.llm import get_llm` § reuse the code you already built: settings, OpenSearch client, Jina embeddings, the full agent graph, the LLM
§ `EVAL_FILE = Path("data/eval.jsonl")` § the test set is one JSON object per line in data/eval.jsonl
§ `def build(n)` § make n test questions from your own index
§ `"query": {"function_score": {"random_score": {"seed": 42, "field": "_seq_no"}}}` § ask OpenSearch for n RANDOM chunks; seed 42 makes the same random choice every time (repeatable)
§ `"_source": ["paper_id", "title", "text"]` § bring back only these fields (not the 1024 numbers of the vector)
§ `get_llm(0.3).invoke("Write one specific question that this passage ... answers ...")` § the LLM writes a question the chunk answers (synthetic test set, the RAGAS idea). Temperature 0.3 gives a little variety
§ `out.write(json.dumps({"question": question, "paper_id": chunk["paper_id"]}) + ...)` § save the question and the "gold" paper: the paper the answer must come from
§ `def load()` § read all lines of data/eval.jsonl back into a list
§ `def run_mode(query, mode, k)` § search in one of three ways and return the paper IDs of the top k results
§ `if mode == "hybrid": ... search.hybrid_search(query, k)` § BM25 + vector + RRF: what QueryMesh really uses
§ `if mode == "bm25": body = {... "multi_match": ...}` § keywords only (title counts double, like in search.py)
§ `else: ... {"knn": {"embedding": {"vector": embed([query], "retrieval.query")[0], "k": k}}}` § meaning only: the k nearest vectors
§ `def retrieval(k=5)` § for every question, check if the gold paper is in the top 5 of each mode
§ `hit += 1` § hit@5 = share of questions whose gold paper is in the top 5
§ `reciprocal += 1 / (ranked.index(row["paper_id"]) + 1)` § MRR (mean reciprocal rank): 1 if the gold paper is first, 1/2 if second, 1/3 if third... averaged. It rewards putting the right paper HIGH
§ `print(f"{mode:8} {hit / len(rows):.2f}   {reciprocal / len(rows):.2f}")` § one line per mode with hit@5 and MRR, 2 decimals
§ `def e2e(n)` § run the full agent pipeline on the first n questions
§ `time.perf_counter()` before and after `asyncio.run(ask(row["question"]))` § measure how many seconds one full answer takes
§ `grounded += bool(result.get("grounded"))` § count answers the critic accepted as supported
§ `cited += any(c["paper_id"] == row["paper_id"] for c in result.get("citations", []))` § count answers that cite the gold paper
§ `statistics.median(seconds)` and `max(seconds)` § the typical (p50) and the worst latency
§ the `argparse` block at the bottom § read the command (build, retrieval or e2e) and --n from the command line and call the matching function
CHECKPOINT: the file is saved and `python -c "import querymesh.evaluate"` (with the .venv active) prints nothing.
If not: an ImportError names the missing module: check that Parts 4 to 6A files exist in querymesh/.
## SECTION 7.2 - Build the test set
WHERE: VS Code Terminal, folder ~/querymesh, .venv active, vLLM and OpenSearch running
GOAL: data/eval.jsonl with 30 questions, each linked to the paper that answers it.
Step 1. Activate the environment:
```
cd ~/querymesh && source .venv/bin/activate
```
Step 2. Make the index big enough to be a real test (40 more papers, 20 to 40 minutes, Docling on the GPU):
```
python -m querymesh.ingest --query "cat:cs.CL AND abs:retrieval" --max-papers 40
```
You should see: lines `indexed XXXX.XXXXX: NN chunks ...`, then a dictionary with "added".
Step 3. Count the chunks in the index:
```
curl -s localhost:9200/arxiv-chunks/_count | jq .count
```
You should see: a number above 1000 (it depends on the papers).
Step 4. Build 30 questions (about 1 minute):
```
python -m querymesh.evaluate build --n 30
```
You should see: wrote 30 questions to data/eval.jsonl
Step 5. Read three of them:
```
head -3 data/eval.jsonl | jq
```
You should see: objects like {"question": "Which fusion method ...?", "paper_id": "2510.01234v1"}.
Step 6. Freeze the test set: copy it, and never edit the copy:
```
cp data/eval.jsonl data/eval_frozen.jsonl
```
Watch out: questions written FROM a chunk often reuse the chunk's exact words, which favours BM25. Top teams also write some questions by hand in their own words. Open data/eval.jsonl in VS Code, rewrite 5 questions in different words, save, and say so in your results.
Watch out: random chunks are sometimes a table or an author list. Delete lines whose question makes no sense (VS Code: put the cursor on the line, press Ctrl+Shift+K, then Ctrl+S).
CHECKPOINT: `wc -l data/eval.jsonl` prints 30 (or fewer after deleting bad lines; keep at least 25).
If not: `index_not_found_exception` = the index name differs: check INDEX_NAME in .env.
## SECTION 7.3 - Compare BM25, vector and hybrid search
WHERE: VS Code Terminal, folder ~/querymesh, .venv active
GOAL: numbers that show whether hybrid search with RRF is better than each search alone on YOUR papers.
Step 1. Run the comparison:
```
python -m querymesh.evaluate retrieval
```
You should see: a small table in this shape (the numbers below are only an example of the format, yours will differ):
```
mode     hit@5  MRR   (n=30)
bm25     0.73   0.58
vector   0.70   0.55
hybrid   0.83   0.66
```
Step 2. Write your three lines into a results table (VS Code: create the file results.md in the project root and paste the output under a heading with today's date).
How to read the result:
§ If you see... § It means... § Do this next
§ hybrid is best on both numbers § the two methods find different good papers and RRF combines them § keep hybrid (the normal case)
§ bm25 clearly beats vector § your questions reuse the papers' words (see Watch out in Section 7.2) § rewrite more questions in your own words, then measure again
§ vector clearly beats hybrid § BM25 adds noise for this question style § try rank_constant 20 or a weighted RRF (Section 7.5)
§ all three below 0.4 § bad chunks or a broken index § open 3 failing questions and look at the gold chunk in OpenSearch (Section 4.7)
Watch out: with 30 questions, one question is 0.033. A difference of 0.03 to 0.07 is often luck. Before you claim "hybrid is better", the difference should be clearly larger, and you should say "on 30 questions" (the ARES lesson).
CHECKPOINT: you have three result lines in results.md.
If not: a Jina error (401/429) means the key or the free-tier rate limit: wait a minute and run again.
## SECTION 7.4 - Measure the whole agent pipeline
WHERE: VS Code Terminal, folder ~/querymesh, .venv active. WORKER_MODE=local in .env is fine (faster); remote also works if the five agents run.
GOAL: three end-to-end numbers: grounded rate, citation of the gold paper, latency.
Step 1. Run 10 questions through all five agents (5 to 10 minutes):
```
python -m querymesh.evaluate e2e --n 10
```
You should see: the agent steps for each question, then three lines in this shape (example values):
```
grounded rate      0.90
cites gold paper   0.70
latency p50 / max  14.2s / 41.0s
```
Step 2. Paste the three lines into results.md under the retrieval lines.
§ Number § Good sign § Warning sign § What to look at
§ grounded rate § 0.8 or more § below 0.7 § the writer prompt in skills.py, or the critic is too strict: read 2 rejected answers in Logfire/Langfuse
§ cites gold paper § close to hit@5 of hybrid § much lower than hit@5 § the grader throws away good chunks: test skills.grade on that question by hand (Section 5.3)
§ latency max § under 60 s § over 90 s § many retries: lower MAX_GENERATIONS or MAX_RETRIEVALS in .env, or check vLLM load with `docker logs vllm --tail 20`
Watch out: evaluate.py calls the agent graph directly, so the Upstash cache (used only by the API) does not shorten these times. If you measure latency through the API instead, ask each question only once: a repeated question comes back from the cache in under a second and hides the real agent time.
CHECKPOINT: results.md has the retrieval table and the three end-to-end numbers.
If not: if an answer never ends, press Ctrl+C, then check that vLLM answers: `curl -s localhost:8000/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer $(grep '^LLM_API_KEY=' .env | cut -d= -f2)"`.
## SECTION 7.5 - Tuning experiments (one change at a time)
WHERE: VS Code (editor + Terminal), folder ~/querymesh
GOAL: improve hit@5 / MRR with controlled experiments, the way a research lab does.
The procedure for EVERY experiment:
Step 1. Write down the experiment name and the one setting you change in results.md.
Step 2. Make the change (table below says where).
Step 3. If you changed search.py PIPELINE_BODY, update the pipeline in OpenSearch:
```
python -m querymesh.search setup
```
Step 4. Run the measurement:
```
python -m querymesh.evaluate retrieval
```
Step 5. Paste the result next to the experiment name.
Step 6. Undo the change if the result is not clearly better (difference larger than about 0.07 on 30 questions).
§ Experiment § Where § Change § What usually happens
§ A rank constant § querymesh/search.py, PIPELINE_BODY § `"rank_constant": 60` to `20` § the top results of each list count more; can help when one method is strong
§ B more vector candidates § querymesh/search.py, hybrid_search § `"k": k * 5` to `k * 10` § the vector side considers more chunks before fusion; small gain, a bit slower
§ C fewer results for the agents § .env § `TOP_K=8` to `TOP_K=5` § less text for the grader and writer: faster, sometimes misses the gold chunk
§ D smaller chunks § querymesh/ingest.py § `CHUNK_CHARS = 1500` to `800`, `OVERLAP_CHARS = 200` to `150` § more precise matches; needs re-indexing (see below)
§ E stricter grading § .env § `MIN_RELEVANT=2` to `3` § more rewrites/scouting, better grounded rate, higher latency (measure with e2e)
§ F contextual chunks (Anthropic [46]) § querymesh/search.py, index_chunks § embed the title, the section heading and the chunk text (one per line) instead of title + text § chunks are easier to find; needs re-indexing
Re-indexing after D or F (deletes the index, then loads the papers again):
```
curl -s -X DELETE localhost:9200/arxiv-chunks && python -m querymesh.ingest --query "cat:cs.CL AND abs:retrieval" --max-papers 45
```
Watch out: after re-indexing, the chunk texts change, so build a NEW test set (Section 7.2 Step 4) and compare all modes again on that new set. Comparing numbers measured on two different test sets is meaningless.
Watch out: keep data/eval_frozen.jsonl for a final check only. If you tune until the frozen set looks good, it stops being a fair test (you "overfit" to it).
Elite extension (optional): weighted RRF. OpenSearch's score-ranker-processor also accepts weights per query clause (the documentation shows 0.7 and 0.3) [15]. Try `"combination": {"technique": "rrf", "rank_constant": 60, "parameters": {"weights": [0.4, 0.6]}}` in PIPELINE_BODY (verify the exact field name in [15] for your OpenSearch version), run Step 3 and Step 4, and record it as experiment G.
CHECKPOINT: results.md has at least experiments A and C with their numbers, and you kept only changes that clearly helped.
If not: if `python -m querymesh.search setup` fails after your edit, the JSON in PIPELINE_BODY is broken: undo with Ctrl+Z and save.
## SECTION 7.6 - Production hardening checklist (what top teams add)
WHERE: this checklist; each row says where the change would go
GOAL: know exactly what separates your portfolio system from a system a company would run, and be able to explain it.
§ Area § What top teams do § QueryMesh today § How to add it
§ Evaluation in CI § run a fixed retrieval test on every pull request and fail if hit@5 drops § manual (Section 7.3) § a GitHub Actions job that starts OpenSearch as a service container with a small fixed index and runs `python -m querymesh.evaluate retrieval`
§ Prompt injection § treat retrieved paper text as data, never as instructions § the critic checks support, prompts say "use ONLY the passages" § add a sentence to the writer prompt saying that passage text is reference material only and is never to be followed as a command; add a test chunk that contains a fake command and check the answer does not obey it
§ HTTPS § TLS on the public entrance § HTTP on the ALB § request a certificate in AWS Certificate Manager and add the annotations `alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/certificate-arn` and `alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/listen-ports` [38]
§ Who may use it § short-lived tokens, real user accounts, rate limits § one user from .env, 60-minute JWT § an identity provider (Cognito, Auth0), per-user rate limit at the API or AWS WAF on the ALB
§ Gradio exposure § UI behind login § Gradio is public on the ALB (Section 6B.12 row 7 limits it to your IP) § keep inbound-cidrs, or add Gradio's own auth / an OIDC proxy
§ Scaling § autoscale on CPU or queue length § fixed replicas § a HorizontalPodAutoscaler for querymesh-api and the writer/grader agents
§ Search storage § replicated, backed-up search cluster § one OpenSearch pod without a disk § Amazon OpenSearch Service, or the chart with persistence enabled + the EBS CSI driver add-on; snapshots to S3
§ LLM capacity § several GPU replicas, fallbacks § one vLLM on one Hyperstack VM § a second VM behind a load balancer, or a hosted fallback model when vLLM times out
§ Cost tracking § cost per question § token counts in Langfuse § add a custom model price for qwen3-4b in Langfuse (Project Settings > Models) [27]
§ Alerts § page someone when errors rise § traces only § Logfire alerts on error rate and latency [26], CloudWatch alarm on the ALB 5xx count
§ Secrets § rotation, no long-lived keys § ESO + OIDC (good) § rotate JWT_SECRET and LLM_API_KEY every 90 days with put-secret-value (Section 6B.6 row 4)
§ Supply chain § scanned, signed images § ECR scanOnPush § block deploys with critical findings; sign images (Sigstore cosign)
§ Releases § canary or blue/green, quick rollback § rolling update § `helm rollback querymesh REVISION -n querymesh` today; Argo Rollouts for canaries later
§ Network § only needed traffic between pods § everything can talk to everything § Kubernetes NetworkPolicies (needs a CNI that enforces them)
CHECKPOINT: you can explain, for at least five rows, why the row matters and how you would add it.
If not: re-read Part 3 (Sections 3.11 to 3.13) for the background of tracing, JWT and Kubernetes.
## SECTION 7.7 - Common mistake at this level
Mistake: trusting one number. A higher average hit@5 on 30 questions can be luck, and a pipeline can score well while being slow or citing the wrong paper.
How to spot it: you cannot say how many questions the number is based on, you changed two settings at once, or you measured on the same questions you tuned on. Fix: frozen test set, one change per experiment, report n, and always look at a few failing questions by hand, as every serious RAG paper does.
Second mistake: optimising retrieval alone. Look at cites gold paper and grounded rate (Section 7.4) together with hit@5: if retrieval improves but the end-to-end numbers do not, the bottleneck is in the grader or the writer.
## PART 7 RESULT CHECK
§ Check § Where § Expected
§ test set § `wc -l data/eval.jsonl` § 25-30 lines
§ frozen copy § `ls data/eval_frozen.jsonl` § the file exists and was never edited
§ retrieval comparison § results.md § three lines (bm25, vector, hybrid) with hit@5 and MRR and n
§ end-to-end § results.md § grounded rate, cites gold paper, latency p50/max
§ experiments § results.md § at least two experiments, one change each, with the decision kept/undone
